# 3. How your project can be unique compared with the 20 research papers

Your literature survey already covers statistical forecasting, machine learning, deep learning, Transformer-based methods, hybrid models, supply-chain resilience, commodity arrivals, remote sensing and inventory management.

So you should not claim that your project is unique simply because it uses AI, predicts prices for 90 days, combines multiple algorithms, or displays a dashboard. These ideas already appear in related research or existing market-intelligence systems.

Your strongest proposed contribution is to connect the forecasting results to a practical, regional government decision workflow.

| Feature              | Existing research and systems in your survey                               | Your proposed contribution                                                                                |
| -------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Price forecasting    | Many papers use statistical, ML or DL models                               | Compare models across 7-, 30- and 90-day horizons, and display performance for each commodity and market  |
| External factors     | Some studies investigate weather, crop conditions, arrivals or disruptions | Combine only the reliable, available factors into a consistent regional forecasting pipeline              |
| Regional monitoring  | Market data and some studies examine individual markets or regions         | Connect forecasts, regional risk and verified inventory records in one workflow                           |
| Government decisions | Some research addresses supply-chain resilience or inventory               | Convert forecast-based alerts into a transparent supply-gap assessment and proposed buffer-stock quantity |
| Decision tracking    | Not established as a common feature across all the summaries you provided  | Track alert → verification → proposed action → official approval → recorded outcome                       |
| Reliability          | Research compares forecasting models                                       | Show model errors, data freshness, uncertainty and reasons for insufficient evidence                      |

This comparison is based on the summaries you shared. It is not proof that no other system offers these features. Before finalizing your journal paper's novelty claim, you should compare the full papers and relevant official systems.

## The specific novelty I recommend

Call it a Forecast-to-Action Buffer-Stock Decision Support Framework.

Its workflow would be:

1\. Updated market data

Prices, market arrivals, weather and other available factors

2\. Multi-horizon forecasting

Separate 7-, 30- and 90-day forecasts

3\. Regional risk assessment

Price change, forecast uncertainty and potential local supply gap

4\. Buffer-stock recommendation

Check verified stock and estimate a possible quantity when sufficient data exists

5\. Official review and action tracking

Human approval, recorded action and subsequent monitoring

The research question becomes: Can connecting multi-horizon price forecasting with regional supply-gap assessment and transparent buffer-stock decision support provide more useful intervention recommendations than price forecasting alone?

You can test this through forecast accuracy, quality of risk alerts, accuracy of supply-gap estimates when actual data is available, and the usefulness of the decision workflow. Do not claim it reduces prices or prevents shortages unless your evaluation demonstrates that result.

# 4. Which algorithms should you use?

Start with a small, reliable set rather than using every algorithm mentioned in your literature survey.

| Task                        | Recommended method                                                       | Purpose                                                                          |
| --------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------ | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Baseline forecasting        | Seasonal Naive                                                           | Provides a simple benchmark to beat                                              |
| Statistical forecasting     | SARIMA, if seasonality is supported by the data                          | Models historical time patterns                                                  |
| Main ML forecasting         | XGBoost or Random Forest                                                 | Learns relationships from lagged prices and available external features          |
| Optional advanced model     | LSTM or Transformer                                                      | Compare only if you have enough suitable historical data and time to evaluate it |
| Price anomaly detection     | Statistical thresholds initially; Isolation Forest if justified          | Flags unusual observed price movements for review                                |
| Regional risk               | Validated rules based on expected price changes and forecast uncertainty | Prioritizes markets needing attention                                            |
| Release-quantity estimation | Demand–supply gap calculation with stock and policy constraints          | Estimates a candidate intervention quantity                                      |

You do not need all these algorithms in the first version. I recommend Seasonal Naive + SARIMA + XGBoost as your initial comparison. Choose the best-performing approach using chronological validation and report results separately for each forecast horizon. If XGBoost does not outperform the simpler models, use the model that is best supported by your results.

For new or unseen data, your system should validate the input, fetch relevant historical records, check that the required features exist and use a model that has been tested on comparable markets. If a new commodity has no adequate history, show that a reliable forecast is unavailable. Do not promise accurate predictions for completely unseen conditions without testing.

# 5. Technology stack for an implementable website

Keep the first version manageable.

Frontend — React

Build the home page, role-based dashboards, forecast charts, filters, regional map, alerts and inventory tables. Use a chart library such as Recharts or Plotly and Leaflet for the map.

Backend — Python + FastAPI

Create APIs for commodity data, forecasts, regional risk, inventory, recommendations, alerts and user roles.

Forecasting — Pandas, scikit-learn, XGBoost, Statsmodels

Clean price records, create features, train and evaluate models, and generate the three forecast horizons.

Database — PostgreSQL

Store market observations, forecast results, source-update history, inventory records, alerts, proposed recommendations and official action logs.

For market prices, start by checking the official [AGMARKNET portal](https://agmarknet.gov.in/?utm_source=chatgpt.com) and the Government of India's [daily mandi-price dataset](https://data.gov.in/resource/current-daily-price-various-commodities-various-markets-mandi?utm_source=chatgpt.com). Verify the available access method, data fields, update frequency and any API requirements before coding the integration.

Weather, crop production, market arrivals and buffer-stock records may come from different sources and may not all be available through live APIs. Design the backend to handle each source separately. Display the last successful update, and never label old records as real-time data.

# 6. A practical implementation order

Build the project in this order so you can demonstrate a working application early.

1. Create the interface: home page, login, farmer dashboard and government dashboard, initially using clearly labelled sample data.
2. Build the database: commodities, markets, observed prices, forecasts, inventory, alerts and action logs.
3. Connect price data: import historical records, validate the data, remove duplicates and store source timestamps.
4. Build forecasting: train and evaluate the baseline, SARIMA and XGBoost models; generate 7-, 30- and 90-day forecasts.
5. Add market comparison: show observed prices and forecasts by commodity and location.
6. Add regional risk: implement documented thresholds and show why a market has been flagged.
7. Build buffer-stock review: allow authorized users to verify inventory and view a proposed supply-gap estimate when enough data exists.
8. Add approvals and alerts: track recommendations and decisions without automatically issuing government stock-release instructions.
9. Test unseen data: evaluate on held-out time periods and markets; handle missing data, stale records and unsupported commodities.
10. Deploy and document: secure the login, protect government records, record model versions and provide a clear explanation of the system's limitations.

## My final recommendation

Build one website with two main dashboards, not two separate applications. Keep the farmer experience focused on price forecasts and cultivation or selling decisions. Make the government experience focused on regional risk, verified buffer-stock availability and transparent intervention recommendations.

The most important feature to implement well is the link between 90-day price-risk forecasting and location-specific buffer-stock planning. That gives your project a stronger and more defensible research direction than simply combining several forecasting algorithms into a dashboard.